#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c241ca2c-bbf5-5377-99ef-435cbc377753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315101} {:answer/id #uuid "567f12c8-c922-5d6d-a930-99483ea9ef1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315102} {:answer/id #uuid "9741e3d2-fd7b-5558-b19b-bdf48cbd60cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315103} {:answer/id #uuid "2f228185-df63-5c8b-98a0-43513296937c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315104} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8e6d-716f-51cb-a80f-fd19f36cadd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8e6d-716f-51cb-a80f-fd19f36cadd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315105} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e2e4c4f-5dd7-5f99-a0fe-b40a225fa0bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56b858e8-c4fa-5c2c-84ef-c3854e0367d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126875" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa61e374-3a73-5b17-9eb5-3eae45f312c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa61e374-3a73-5b17-9eb5-3eae45f312c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330115} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae3ef6e-ab2e-5ade-ad19-cd3a59e37fff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9070d2fc-ef35-5217-a380-97c0b5a05359" :question/math-academy-id "q-347637" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x+{sec}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ){tan}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2) & ≈-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09b2459-e7e0-5b55-a80a-e3ec777a3b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a09b2459-e7e0-5b55-a80a-e3ec777a3b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330118} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2db4285-6cd5-5770-a25e-7d88e563f7f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ffbebfb-f837-5e6c-b95e-3d4c9ef7df85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ffbebfb-f837-5e6c-b95e-3d4c9ef7df85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330120} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8808fb-ebd6-5821-8758-222afaa6e475" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4289a5e-3f0c-59c8-b77d-091d5a143d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4289a5e-3f0c-59c8-b77d-091d5a143d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330122} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd3f18b-8e59-5ad0-bab0-fc4fce9c9fee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab2ebb5a-5574-563b-a19e-a919adb7c6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-353859" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x{e}^{x+2}$ about $x=-1.$\n\n$x{e}^{x+2}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+1)^{3}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x{e}^{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x{e}^{x+2} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{1}=-e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+x{e}^{x+2}=(1+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(1-1){e}^{1}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(1+x){e}^{x+2}=(2+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(2-1){e}^{1}=e \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(2+x){e}^{x+2}=(3+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =(3-1){e}^{1}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{x+2} & =-e+(0)(x+1)+\\frac{e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{2e}{3!}(x+1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-e+\\frac{e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{e}{3}(x+1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f7733a-e203-5955-a0c4-4651f483748d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315122} {:answer/id #uuid "4769134a-6e2d-5876-ba70-e6374cc2431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1e193b-b2cd-570e-9408-725025ccf94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315124} {:answer/id #uuid "46a98f0c-90ce-5056-b47d-e43ad80d0bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315125} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb24355-b6fc-5261-a43e-938374eab8b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-\\frac{(x+2)}{2}+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4769134a-6e2d-5876-ba70-e6374cc2431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c71e486-aef9-537f-8515-b3787190972b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7afe118-2a0b-56b6-9c4b-c1f500a7e109" :question/math-academy-id "q-83185" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c0c571-c530-53f8-84be-f381739b9e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.792\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330125} {:answer/id #uuid "a496d11a-3260-56c7-b8cc-a7b01df5f213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.764\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330126} {:answer/id #uuid "c73e0d91-6e95-53f3-a811-152fe40e0643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.787\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330127} {:answer/id #uuid "8beb57a2-9a93-5617-8158-fbe8681cc374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.732\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330128} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d34c65-1180-532f-bd46-bc08e5eab51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d34c65-1180-532f-bd46-bc08e5eab51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330129} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cd1b69-97b5-50fb-bf77-12efcb36b6cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43923f1d-dfe1-5ab3-936a-6fc1d8791c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-9623" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ Give your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{1/2}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2+\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}})(x-4)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-4}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{64}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi $ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } & ≈2+\\frac{\\pi -4}{4}-\\frac{(\\pi -4)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =1.773\\,9\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}]}
